A||||9||Fformula88||New brakes... WOW||||10||
Z||000000||Fformula88||12-20-2001||01:29 PM||||I was able to drive my Fiero for the first time in two weeks last night after finally completing my new front brake job, and all I have to say is WOW.<P>I have owned the car for 5 years, and could not figure out why everyone thought the 88 brakes were so much better than the 84-87.  My Formula didn't seem to stop any better.<P>At the beginning of this month, the righ front caliper froze open, so when I braked the left wheel would lock and the car would dive to the left.  I tore it apart, and ordered rebuild kits and pistons (old ones were rusty). <P>With the parts here, and the calipers sand blasted and painted silver in the meantime I was able to put it back together and simply cannot believe how well the car stops.  The old calipers must have been partially frozen from the day I bought the car!  These brakes are awesome compared to how they were, and I now can see why they are a step up on the old ones!||||reg||10
Z||000001||FieroJoe||12-20-2001||02:23 PM||||Besides the calipers being different, the lack of fade on the 88 coupe that I've driven (FieroMaster88's) is wonderful compared to my 87 coupe's.  The vented rotors help out so much.  Even on just a hot day I can feel my brakes not working as well.<P>I just wish I had the cash to upgrade my suspension/brakes (saving for engine swap, '87 V6)<P>------------------<BR>   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierojoesig2.gif">  <BR>'87 Coupe 5spd.<BR>AIM:FieroJoe87||||reg||1
Z||000002||artherd||12-21-2001||05:02 AM||||Yeah, the 88s have a tendency to sieze on their slider pins.<P>Infact, out of 8 calipers on my two 88s, I have personally siezed SIX!  New pins, and grease, in; and I haven't siezed one I've rebuilt yet.<P><BR>The 88 brakes are definately better, but not really great by any streatch of the imagination.<P>I can still heat mine to the point where they will just NOT lock the wheels, even with me stopming on the pedal with both feet.  Sigh.<P>(Agip DOT 5.1 fluid (glycol, not dot5 sil.)  SS braided lines, Carbonmet pads, and crossdrilled rotors (same price as plain rotors, figured what the hell I'd try to crack 'em around the holes if I can  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif"><P>I do not think I am boiling this fluid, or the ATE Super Blue I had in there previously.  Both have dry boil temps in the 500+F range.<P>Of course, this only happens on the autocross track, or if I'm really pushing the car... which is when I really NEED the bloody brakes...<P>Best!<BR>Ben.<P>------------------<BR>      <IMG SRC="http://www.cartsys.com/fiero/sig1.jpeg">    <BR>Ben Cannon<BR>88 Formula, T-top, Metalic Red           &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;"Every Man Dies, not every man really Lives"<BR>88 Formula, Northstar, Silver                    &nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;&nbsp;-Mel Gibson, "Braveheart"||||reg||3
Z||000003||Master_Sushi||12-21-2001||01:29 PM||||Fiero 88 brakes are Much better then the pre88. On my 88 coupe I only had them smoking once, this was after a few "very quick" 80-0 mph stops. I mean "very quick" as in slaming on brakes as hard as possable from 85+mph to the point of almost locking up my wide 16 inch Kumhos. It was funny when it happend, I pulled up to a red light when they were cooking. Smoke was coming out of every wheel, it looked like my fiero was on fire(haven't heard that before   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> ). It drew some attention to say the least.<P>On my 84 coupe, if I just do one quick stop I can tell they are geting hot. Doing a few quick stops will cause them to get smelly and after a while start smoking. I do like how cheap pads and such are for them. If I ever get a pre88 fiero I really care about, I might try crossdrilled rotors and performance pads. I'm hoping that helps a *bit*.<p>[This message has been edited by Master_Sushi (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||14
Z||000004||btoth||12-21-2001||04:12 PM||||Ever since I bought my car I thought the brakes just sucked naturally.  For Xmas I got an early gift of steel brake lines.  OH MY GOD!  The difference over the old worn out rubber lines is amazing.  Now the pedal sits much higher and takes little effort to brake.  I am also finally able to slam my brakes and lock them up if need be.  <P>If any of you have a soft or rubbery pedal and your braking takes a lot of effort to stop fast, get new lines!  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">||||reg||1
Z||000005||theogre||12-21-2001||08:28 PM||||FF88, working brakes are always an improvement.  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif"> <P>Ben, If you boiled the fluid, you'd know... usually no brakes at all when that happens. Just about the same effect as poping a line. What you are discribing is a classic fade problem.<P>------------------<BR>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/fullstaff.gif">  <FONT COLOR="blue"><I><B>11-Sept-01, The day the world as we knew it ended.</B></I></FONT>||||reg||1
Z||000006||Fformula88||12-21-2001||09:41 PM||||Yeah, I'll say working brakes are an improvement.<P>My sliders were working fine too.  It was the main cylinder that had frozen up.  The pistons had started to corrode.  I never knew they weren't working well until one stopped working at all!||||reg||1
Z||000007||Fierokid87||12-21-2001||10:44 PM||||Is the GA/beretta break upgrade recomended then?  I mean ill loose my e-break but is it worth it?<P>Scott<P>------------------<BR><FONT COLOR="red"> Fierokid's  Web site:</FONT><BR><FONT COLOR="white">http://www.angelfire.com/sports/sfiero</FONT><P><FONT COLOR="blue">AIM: Fierokid87<BR>ICQ: Fierokid</FONT><P>  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/uploads/fierokid.gif"> ||||reg||1
Z||000008||pherder||12-21-2001||11:04 PM||||  <BLOCKQUOTE><font size="1" face="Verdana, Arial, Helvetica">quote:</font><HR>Originally posted by Fformula88:<BR><B>I tore it apart, and ordered rebuild kits and pistons (old ones were rusty). <P></B><HR></BLOCKQUOTE><P>Fformula88, where did you get the pistons (part numbers?)  GM part number 3487694?<P>I have my fronts apart and the pistons have rust rot on them also so I need to replace them.<P>TIA!<P>Paul<BR><p>[This message has been edited by pherder (edited 12-21-2001).]||||reg||5
Z||000009||Fformula88||12-22-2001||11:28 AM||||I ordered them thru Napa.  Not sure what the part numbers were on them, but just go to  <A HREF="http://www.napaonline.com" TARGET=_blank>www.napaonline.com</A>  plunk in your car and search for them.  They are there.  Down side is it took about a week to get them here, but the plus side was it only cost me about $35 to get both sides.  I saw them cheaper on a website based parts place, but they reported shipment wouldn't happen for 10-15 days, I didn't want to wait that long.<P>I do not think the GA upgrade is recommended, theogre is certainly the one to ask!  Also do a search in the tech section, there was a huge thread discussing whether the GA upgrade was dangerous or not a while back.||||reg||1
